# yopl — AI Agent Rules

## Project identity

`yopl` is an ES6 mini-library that implements a logic solver in the style of Prolog. It provides
rule-based logic programming with both callback and generator styles. Its only runtime dependency
is [`deep6`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/deep6) (a zero-dependency library) for unification.

## Critical rules

- **ES6 modules.** The project is `"type": "module"`. Use `import`/`export` syntax.
- **Single runtime dependency.** Only `deep6` is allowed in `dependencies`. Do not add others.
- **Do not modify or delete test expectations** without understanding why they changed.
- **Do not add or remove comments** unless explicitly asked.
- **Keep `src/` in sync.** Run `npm test` and `npm run lint:fix` after changes.

## Code style

- 2-space indentation.
- Single quotes for strings.
- Semicolons are required (enforced by Prettier).
- See `.prettierrc` for formatting settings.

## Architecture quick reference

- **Main entry:** `src/solve.js` — solver core.
- **Solvers:** `src/solvers/` — alternative solver drivers.
  - `gen.js` — synchronous generator-based solver.
  - `async.js` — async callback-based solver.
  - `asyncGen.js` — async generator-based solver.
- **Rules:** `src/rules/` — built-in rule modules.
  - `logic.js` — logical connectives.
  - `comp.js` — comparison rules.
  - `math.js` — arithmetic rules.
  - `bits.js` — bitwise rules.
  - `system.js` — system/utility rules.

## Verification commands

- `npm test` — run the test suite.
- `npm run debug` — run tests with Node inspector.
- `npm run ts-check` — TypeScript type checking.
- `npm run lint` — Prettier check.
- `npm run lint:fix` — Prettier write.

## File layout

- Entry point: `src/solve.js`
- Solvers: `src/solvers/` (gen.js, async.js, asyncGen.js)
- Rules: `src/rules/` (logic.js, comp.js, math.js, bits.js, system.js)
- Tests: `tests/tests.js` (dispatcher), grouped in `tests/test-*.js`

## When reading the codebase

- Start with `ARCHITECTURE.md` for the module map and dependency graph.
- `src/solve.js` is the core solver — read it first.
- `src/solvers/` contains alternative driver styles built on the same core.
- `src/rules/` defines the built-in rule library.
- Read `README.md` for usage examples.
